@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    REL-150  Cut 1.5.0. Changelog, error map and the feature flag
             fixture all need tidying before the tag goes on.

Three files, open as three buffers.

  1. CHANGELOG.md — everything under `Unreleased` moves under the empty
     `## 1.5.0` heading, sorted alphabetically, with a blank line before
     `## 1.4.0` as every section has. The `Bump dev dependencies` entry
     is dropped; we do not log those. `Add customer tiers` is also
     listed under 1.4.0 by mistake, where it never shipped. Remove that
     one. `Unreleased` is left as an empty section.

  2. errors.ts — four codes were added at the bottom this sprint. The
     map is kept in status code order, lowest first. Codes that tie
     keep their current order.

  3. flags.fixture.ts — `customer-tiers` and `partial-refunds` only
     exist for staging. Production needs both, identical apart from
     `env`, added at the end of the list in the same order.

Nothing in this ticket needs the cursor to leave line 1 of any file.

@keys
| `:7d` | delete line 7 |
| `:5,9m11` | move lines 5 to 9 to below line 11 |
| `:7,10sort` | sort lines 7 to 10 |
| `:2,9sort n /pat/` | sort by the number after the match |
| `:2,3t5` | copy lines 2 to 3 to below line 5 |
| `:6,7s/a/b/` | substitute on lines 6 to 7 only |
| `:bn` `:wqa` | next buffer; write all and quit |

@hints
In the changelog, delete first, then move, then sort, then delete the
1.4.0 duplicate. Each step changes the line numbers for the next, so
read the gutter again before every command rather than planning all
four in advance.
--
Move the blank line under the Unreleased list along with the entries.
It becomes the blank line before `## 1.4.0`, and Unreleased keeps the
one above it.
--
The error map has two keys with digits in their names. Sort by the
number after `statusCode`, not the first number on the line.
--
The flags: copy the two lines to the end, then a substitute with a
two-line range on the copies. Par assumes you never retype a flag line.

@solution
A route that measures ninety-seven keystrokes.

**CHANGELOG.md** — `:7d` drops the dependency bump. `:5,9m11` moves the
four entries and their trailing blank line under `## 1.5.0`. They are
now lines 7 to 10, so `:7,10sort`. The stray tiers entry under 1.4.0 is
now on line 16: `:16d`.

**errors.ts** — `:bn`, then `:2,9sort n /statusCode/`.

**flags.fixture.ts** — `:bn`, `:2,3t5` to copy the staging pair to the
end of the list, then `:6,7s/staging/production/` on the copies. `:wqa`.

Every command in this ticket is a range, a verb and an argument, and
not one of them needed the cursor to move. That is the shape of the ex
language and it is what you should feel when a job is mostly moving
lines around: the file has coordinates, the gutter shows them, and the
command line takes them directly.

The thing to watch is the coordinates changing under you. Each delete
and move renumbers everything below it. The habit that keeps you safe
is to run one command, look, and read the numbers again for the next
one, rather than working out a sequence in your head. When you want the
numbers not to shift, a `:g` marks every line first and then acts, and
chapter 15 already showed you that trick.

@start CHANGELOG.md
# Changelog

## Unreleased

- Record cancellation reason on orders
- Add partial refunds
- Bump dev dependencies
- Fix duplicate charge on retried checkout
- Add customer tiers

## 1.5.0

## 1.4.0

- Add refund window
- Send email receipts
- Add customer tiers

## 1.3.0

- Add customer lookup by email
- Return 404 for unknown orders

@expected CHANGELOG.md
# Changelog

## Unreleased

## 1.5.0

- Add customer tiers
- Add partial refunds
- Fix duplicate charge on retried checkout
- Record cancellation reason on orders

## 1.4.0

- Add refund window
- Send email receipts

## 1.3.0

- Add customer lookup by email
- Return 404 for unknown orders

